  "caption": "How does a hash map find your value instantly? #programming #coding #computerscience",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "say": "How does a hash map find your value instantly?",
      "visual": {
        "type": "title",
        "kicker": "data structures",
        "title": "How hash maps are instant",
        "subtitle": "O(1) lookups, explained in 40 seconds"
      }
    },
    {
      "say": "Your key goes through a hash function, which turns it into a number. That number picks a bucket.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "diagram",
        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "k",
            "label": "\"apple\"",
            "sub": "key",
            "cue": "key"
          },
          {
            "id": "h",
            "label": "hash()",
            "sub": "\u2192 93021",
            "cue": "hash"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "bucket 5",
            "sub": "93021 % 8",
            "cue": "bucket"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "say": "In code, that's just the hash modulo the number of buckets.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "code",
        "lang": "rust",
        "filename": "map.rs",
        "highlight": [
          3
        ],
        "code": "fn bucket(key: &str, n: usize) -> usize {\n    let h = hash(key);\n    (h % n as u64) as usize\n}"
      }
    },
    {
      "say": "When two keys land in the same bucket, that's a collision. The map keeps a short list and checks each key.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "bullets",
        "title": "Collisions",
        "items": [
          {
            "text": "Two keys, same bucket",
            "cue": "collision"
          },
          {
            "text": "Keep a short list",
            "cue": "list"
          },
          {
            "text": "Compare keys one by one",
            "cue": "checks"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "say": "Too many collisions? It doubles the buckets and rehashes. That's why lookups stay fast.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "stat",
        "value": "O(1)",
        "label": "average lookup",
    { "say": "Follow for more programming explained simply.", "visual": { "type": "outro", "title": "Now you know.", "cta": "Follow for more", "handle": "@yourhandle" } }
  ]
}
